WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.557
File upload features look simple at first.

2
00:00:02.637 --> 00:00:06.693
A Java application accepts a profile
image, stores it somewhere, and returns a link.

3
00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:10.144
Over time, that same pattern often expands
into support attachments,

4
00:00:10.224 --> 00:00:13.823
invoices, resumes, diagnostic archives,
identity documents, previews,

5
00:00:13.903 --> 00:00:17.788
malware scanning, object storage, download
links, and background processing.

6
00:00:17.868 --> 00:00:20.895
At that point, the upload endpoint is only
one piece of the risk.

7
00:00:20.975 --> 00:00:24.403
The full lifecycle includes intake,
validation, temporary handling,

8
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:27.911
storage, processing, access control,
logging, retention, deletion,

9
00:00:27.991 --> 00:00:29.134
and incident response.

10
00:00:29.214 --> 00:00:32.527
Uploads are high-risk input paths because
they let users, partners,

11
00:00:32.607 --> 00:00:36.663
administrators, or integrations place
content into the application environment.

12
00:00:36.743 --> 00:00:39.600
A file may flow through a reverse proxy,
servlet container,

13
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.336
multipart resolver, temporary directory,
Java controller, object store,

14
00:00:43.416 --> 00:00:46.844
malware scanner, image library, document
parser, preview generator,

15
00:00:46.924 --> 00:00:48.695
search indexer, and download route.

16
00:00:48.775 --> 00:00:50.203
Each step can change the risk.

17
00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:53.768
A file that was harmless during intake may
become dangerous when parsed,

18
00:00:53.848 --> 00:00:56.819
exposed publicly, indexed, or served with
the wrong headers.

19
00:00:56.899 --> 00:01:00.098
The practical risks include malicious
content, oversized files,

20
00:01:00.178 --> 00:01:03.834
unsafe filenames, path traversal, public
exposure, unauthorized access,

21
00:01:03.914 --> 00:01:07.913
metadata leakage, storage abuse, resource
exhaustion, parser vulnerabilities,

22
00:01:07.993 --> 00:01:10.679
unsafe post-processing, and sensitive data
exposure.

23
00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:14.072
Java teams may use Spring MVC, Spring
Boot, Jakarta Servlet, JAX-RS,

24
00:01:14.152 --> 00:01:18.094
application servers, reverse proxies,
object storage, and background workers.

25
00:01:18.174 --> 00:01:21.259
Those tools help, but none of them make an
upload safe by default.

26
00:01:21.339 --> 00:01:24.424
The goal is to accept only the files the
business actually needs,

27
00:01:24.504 --> 00:01:26.961
process them safely, store them in the
right place,

28
00:01:27.041 --> 00:01:28.697
and control who can retrieve them.

29
00:01:28.777 --> 00:01:32.548
Secure upload handling is not one
annotation, one filename cleanup function,

30
00:01:32.628 --> 00:01:34.627
one antivirus scan, or one bucket policy.

31
00:01:34.707 --> 00:01:37.792
It is a design that makes allowed use
cases, limits, validation,

32
00:01:37.872 --> 00:01:39.986
storage boundaries, processing behavior,

33
00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.580
and operational response explicit and
reviewable.
